WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.422
Why try negative gains? A verified example.

2
00:00:08.422 --> 00:00:16.111
Six nodes, maxDiff 2, cut 6: every legal move has gain ≤ 0.

3
00:00:16.111 --> 00:00:22.244
Hill climbing stops at 6; FM takes A −1, B −1: cut 8.

4
00:00:22.244 --> 00:00:27.711
Now E's strongest tie is across: +4, cut 4.

5
00:00:27.711 --> 00:00:32.044
Exhaustive search confirms 4 is the balanced optimum.

6
00:00:32.444 --> 00:00:37.022
The attachment had its own example, with a claim.

7
00:00:37.022 --> 00:00:42.711
Computed from its own data: the initial cut is 18, not 9.

8
00:00:42.711 --> 00:00:46.400
E's move is legal, with gain +10.

9
00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:51.200
Pass 1's best and the true optimum are both 8, not 4.

10
00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:55.556
So it stays a regression test, not a lesson.

11
00:00:55.956 --> 00:01:01.422
In real netlists a net often joins three or more cells.

12
00:01:01.422 --> 00:01:07.333
A net {A, B, C, D}, weight 3, with only D on the right.

13
00:01:07.333 --> 00:01:12.356
Counting pins: moving D gains +3, moving A gains 0.

14
00:01:12.356 --> 00:01:17.489
Split into a clique and counted by edges, A would wrongly get −1.

15
00:01:17.889 --> 00:01:21.800
The 1982 paper's speed comes from buckets.

16
00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:28.156
One list per gain, plus a pointer to the largest legal gain.

17
00:01:28.156 --> 00:01:34.489
Each move updates only its neighbours: a pass costs time in proportion to the pins.

18
00:01:34.489 --> 00:01:42.178
This course updates gains incrementally, but still scans to choose: not O(P).

19
00:01:42.578 --> 00:01:46.711
The window counts nodes, not area.

20
00:01:46.711 --> 00:01:52.400
Give A area 3: the accepted ABC | DE is 3 : 2 by count.

21
00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:55.000
By area it is 5 : 2.

22
00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:59.578
Area balance needs its own window: an extension.

23
00:01:59.978 --> 00:02:02.044
Six things to keep.

24
00:02:15.044 --> 00:02:19.178
Next: the goal changes from cut to delay.
